t:700 24px/31px nyt-cheltenham,georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>We=E2=
=80=99ve been here before</span></h2><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geor=
gia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Two rival superpowers. Technolo=
gy that has the capacity to be enormously destructive. A moment of awakenin=
g that reveals just how much danger the world could be in.</span></p><p sty=
le=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span=
>There=E2=80=99s a lot that=E2=80=99s unprecedented about this moment when =
it comes to A.I. But in some respects, we=E2=80=99ve been here before.</spa=
n></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0=
 15px"><span>During the Cold War, the U.S. and the Soviet Union were sworn =
enemies, locked in a race to build ever more, and ever more powerful, nucle=
ar weapons. Then came the Cuban Missile Crisis. Thirteen tense days focused=
 minds, and in the years that followed, the two nations went on to contain =
what was then the gravest known threat to humanity.</span></p><p style=3D"f=
ont:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It=E2=
=80=99s easy to forget that a lot of people didn=E2=80=99t think this was p=
ossible. Today, commentators and </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f=
/newsletter/yGvUaB3cKLH65JzeAw8oOg~~/AAAAARA~/Y3lijM2sLZ3KxaJWm3P1petfl4s1Y=
V8t5mrqj7SVvBiBoy487CJN4ylZ6Tfp7dfQv6dECpSjta1al64w__lhcnyRpCnYAKoafv4qFsyx=
NLL0aqS0z_3RIVBc_5jHgRze89EtBYKxEwC5CgWvufD1C08BfpX3l_bDZNZ10GoC-FbkB2nF2zz=
JYwFB-mDTj8BGCMHVGJY7-ed-msg4X55VD9qmtNPZjUodNSMk2cpLQEzqPu8zwFDcH96QVg-GWw=
6ZFuRMG0pw6dxKs5WRGK1mqF9vZi75InjZD3YI4S6eI8QpeMCaxRPuogPmW1W0Cg6WyYROZHBHF=
7qLnaSWCaYpAndUFDSKGuKPHtFsv2cpk58t1BhgY5H0MkxKbSddXhBA" class=3D"css-sdwaa=
1" style=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-decoration=
:underline">officials are skeptical about an A.I. treaty</a><span> between =
the U.S. and China, too.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgi=
a,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The nuclear analogy goes only so =
far. There are important differences between artificial intelligence and nu=
clear weapons, and some of those might make reaching an agreement to constr=
ain A.I. more difficult. But some could arguably make it easier.</span></p>=
<p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"=
><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Vast differences</span></p><p style=3D"fon=
t:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Artifici=
al intelligence and nuclear weapons are very different technologies.</span>=
</p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 1=
5px"><span>They=E2=80=99re used differently. A.I. is developed by private c=
ompanies, and is effectively in the hands of anyone who has access to a com=
puter or smartphone. Nuclear weapons are =E2=80=A6 not. They=E2=80=99re con=
trolled by governments. Even their civilian equivalent, nuclear power plant=
s, require expensive, highly specialized physical infrastructure. (Though t=
he differences are not as stark as they might seem =E2=80=94 more on that i=
n a bit.)</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#=
333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But perhaps the biggest difference is that nucle=
ar technology was well understood for decades before the world moved to con=
trol the spread of nuclear weapons.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/=
25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>A.I., by contrast, rem=
ains something of a black box. Even America=E2=80=99s frontier labs cannot =
always understand their own A.I. models; indeed, that is one of the worries=
 driving calls to slow down the technology. But it=E2=80=99s not easy to re=
gulate something you don=E2=80=99t understand.</span></p><p style=3D"font:n=
ormal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"fo=
nt-weight:700">Trust but verify 2.0</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/=
25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Still, some principles=
 of nuclear arms control can be applied directly to A.I. For instance, the =
first step toward any agreement between two deeply distrustful parties is t=
o build, well, trust. And the way to do that is to start sharing informatio=
n.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;mar=
gin:0 0 15px"><span>The first thing the U.S. and the Soviet Union did, only=
 months after the Cuban Missile Crisis, was set up a hotline, a 24-hour tel=
ecommunications link from Washington to Moscow. The idea was to give leader=
s a chance to communicate instantaneously to prevent accidental escalation.=
</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margi=
n:0 0 15px"><span>That=E2=80=99s the kind of thing A.I. policy experts say =
could be adapted readily. Connecting not only Washington and Beijing but al=
so the frontier labs would allow all sides to share information about worry=
ing incidents. (In fact, my colleagues </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes=
=2Ecom/f/newsletter/Czd8v3b3I7acdKiEtTXnHw~~/AAAAARA~/SN4TWEwJfbjPYlVSq2yIi=
nSXcHOcfsG_0zQCqxn4-JeMYQ2XH_ggwocLJtZFMcbtL91OaRIp7aZGlN2npCnj-liYt1fX5Pq5=
hY_xd2jUKsUarPjPrnXEH0ATqbTY7ieVthbOR9mrTlG-Y6rmVEVxoGnDwwn3I_gfKC-caS34XqQ=
pDnYmB8Q8etcg2_yF3OTfbxvLGotF6ZMPhmrGsvs_WCTRVrUuexV4qG8Pc65efqlFzILZaRXRZM=
OkcfrL6vsYmRWNEca0Knp0nabF4BOQzfcdyI4_Z__s8X5Xh_w8ZgQ48j3I3-pZwkrHpxGXtTp-L=
8xUdtE0ZBw8nNZBp_f6Luj-qZusmmaVjZSNbjKkUFydrExx43rsOOdwjVBG7KypgXKW_sRediBH=
Ce9yjmHacw~~" class=3D"css-sdwaa1" style=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-deco=
ration:underline;text-decoration:underline">have reported</a><span> that U.=
S. and Chinese officials have already discussed implementing such a system.=
)</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;marg=
in:0 0 15px"><span>Another is a nuclear-style inspection and monitoring reg=
ime. The International Atomic Energy Agency conducts intrusive, in-person i=
nspections of nuclear power plants in many countries to try to ensure nucle=
ar material is used for peaceful purposes.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norma=
l 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Instead of uran=
ium enrichment, A.I. inspectors would monitor, say, caps on compute, the ra=
w processing power that governs how powerful large language models are, sai=
d Anthony Aguirre, chief executive of the Future of Life Institute and an e=
xpert on A.I. safety and governance.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px=
/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>And A.I. might be a w=
idely accessible </span><span style=3D"font-style:italic">tool</span><span>=
, but the data centers that host it are as large-scale and specialized as e=
nrichment facilities; satellites could help with monitoring them too.</span=
></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 =
15px"><span>In one respect, Aguirre said, it might even be easier to keep t=
abs on some A.I. The most advanced A.I. chips are, for now at least, typica=
lly manufactured by one Taiwanese company with machines produced by one Dut=
ch company. In other words, they should be easy to trace. And they already =
have capabilities that can monitor A.I. computing power and be deactivated =
from afar if that compute is tampered with. =E2=80=9CThere=E2=80=99s alread=
y a kill switch, if we choose to use it,=E2=80=9D Aguirre said. =E2=80=9CBu=
